### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer wants to see a bias toward reuse for commodity problems and a clear, evidence-based justification when you do build. Knowing when to buy the safe versus when to build the vault is the test.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class BuildVsBuy {
    enum Decision { BUILD, BUY }
    public static Decision decide(boolean isUnique, boolean isCoreToBusiness) {
        return (isUnique && isCoreToBusiness) ? Decision.BUILD : Decision.BUY;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Retry logic: " + decide(false, false));  // BUY
        System.out.println("Fraud model: " + decide(true, true));    // BUILD
    }
}
```

### What are the five evaluation factors?

Fit: does the existing solution meet the requirement? Time to value: library ships this week, custom takes months. Total cost: custom code is maintained forever. Control: core IP needs ownership. Risk: security functions must use vetted libraries.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.List;
public class FiveFactors {
    record Factor(String name, String buyAdvantage, String buildAdvantage) {}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Factor> factors = List.of(
            new Factor("Fit",           "Meets 90%+ of need",      "Meets 100% of unique need"),
            new Factor("Time to value", "Ships this week",          "Months of development"),
            new Factor("Total cost",    "Community-maintained",     "You maintain forever"),
            new Factor("Control",       "Vendor controls roadmap", "You control everything"),
            new Factor("Risk",          "Vetted security",         "Subtle bugs possible")
        );
        factors.forEach(f -> System.out.println(f.name() + ": BUY=" + f.buyAdvantage() + " BUILD=" + f.buildAdvantage()));
    }
}
```

### When did you choose to reuse at National Bank of India?

Retry logic for flaky downstream payment calls. Battle-tested libraries handle backoff, jitter, and limits correctly. Custom retry code is easy to write badly.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class ReuseExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Reuse: one dependency, proven, community-maintained
        System.out.println("Retry logic: used Spring Retry library");
        System.out.println("Why: commodity problem, battle-tested, zero maintenance");
        System.out.println("Proud of the code I did NOT have to write.");
    }
}
```

### When did you choose to build?

The fraud-scoring rules encode the bank's proprietary risk model. No off-the-shelf product fit more than twenty percent. This is core IP.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class BuildExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fraud model: built from scratch");
        System.out.println("Why: proprietary risk logic, core IP, no product fits > 20%");
        System.out.println("Control: we own the model, can evolve it with our data");
    }
}
```

### Why is the maintenance tail the hidden cost?

Code you write is maintained by you for years: bug fixes, upgrades, on-call burden. Libraries are maintained by their community.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MaintenanceTail {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Year 0: Build cost = 2 weeks");
        System.out.println("Year 1-5: Maintenance cost = 1 week/year x 5 = 5 weeks");
        System.out.println("Total custom cost: 7 weeks over 5 years");
        System.out.println("Library cost: 1 day setup + zero maintenance");
    }
}
```

### Why should you never roll your own crypto?

For security-critical functions, reusing a vetted library is almost always safer than custom code. Subtle mistakes in crypto are catastrophic.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
public class NeverRollYourOwn {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Always use the standard library
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        System.out.println("Use: MessageDigest.getInstance('SHA-256')");
        System.out.println("Never: write your own hash function");
    }
}
```

### What is the best-of-both approach?

Buy the commodity parts and build only the thin unique layer on top. This gives the best time-to-value with the right control.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class PartialReuse {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Commodity (buy): retry, circuit breaker, serialization");
        System.out.println("Unique (build): fraud scoring model, proprietary rules");
        System.out.println("Result: fast delivery + full control where it matters");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "What if the library had a vulnerability?" (we track CVEs and update), "Did you ever regret building?" (only when the maintenance became a burden), "How do you evaluate a library?" (community, security, license, fit).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class BuildBuyFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Library vulnerability? -> Track CVEs, update promptly");
        System.out.println("Regret building? -> Only when maintenance exceeded expectations");
        System.out.println("Evaluate library? -> Community, security history, license, fit");
    }
}
```
